1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,880
What happens when one of the biggest

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:05,130
banks in the world sends almost a

3
00:00:05,130 --> 00:00:07,632
billion dollars to the wrong place,

4
00:00:07,632 --> 00:00:10,416
and the people who receive it say,

5
00:00:10,416 --> 00:00:11,231
thank you,

6
00:00:11,231 --> 00:00:13,237
we are keeping it?

7
00:00:18,483 --> 00:00:19,982
On August eleventh,

8
00:00:19,982 --> 00:00:21,046
twenty twenty,

9
00:00:21,046 --> 00:00:23,435
Citibank meant to pay about seven point

10
00:00:23,435 --> 00:00:25,607
eight million dollars of interest on

11
00:00:25,607 --> 00:00:28,813
behalf of the cosmetics company Revlon.

12
00:00:29,013 --> 00:00:29,716
Instead,

13
00:00:29,716 --> 00:00:31,897
it sent almost nine hundred million

14
00:00:31,897 --> 00:00:34,193
dollars of its own money.

15
00:00:34,353 --> 00:00:36,491
A federal judge later called it one

16
00:00:36,491 --> 00:00:38,240
of the biggest blunders in banking

17
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:39,423
history.

18
00:00:39,653 --> 00:00:41,567
And then he ruled that much of

19
00:00:41,567 --> 00:00:43,480
the money did not have to come

20
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:44,320
back.

21
00:00:49,983 --> 00:00:53,501
Why was Citibank paying Revlon's bills at

22
00:00:53,501 --> 00:00:54,452
all?

23
00:00:58,983 --> 00:01:00,672
In twenty sixteen,

24
00:01:00,672 --> 00:01:03,536
Revlon borrowed one point eight billion

25
00:01:03,536 --> 00:01:06,713
dollars through a syndicated loan.

26
00:01:06,943 --> 00:01:08,323
That means the money did not come

27
00:01:08,323 --> 00:01:08,996
from one bank,

28
00:01:08,996 --> 00:01:10,358
but from dozens of lenders,

29
00:01:10,358 --> 00:01:11,743
mostly investment funds.

30
00:01:11,743 --> 00:01:14,541
Someone has to sit in the middle,

31
00:01:14,541 --> 00:01:17,367
collect Revlon's payments and pass them on

32
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:19,663
to every lender.

33
00:01:19,803 --> 00:01:21,741
That job is called the administrative

34
00:01:21,741 --> 00:01:22,683
agent.

35
00:01:22,683 --> 00:01:25,601
And Revlon's agent was Citibank.

36
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:28,020
Let me draw the plumbing.

37
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:29,810
Here is Revlon.

38
00:01:29,810 --> 00:01:31,023
Here is Citibank,

39
00:01:31,023 --> 00:01:32,100
the agent.

40
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:34,100
And here are the lenders.

41
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:36,651
When an interest payment is due,

42
00:01:36,651 --> 00:01:39,218
Revlon sends the money to Citibank,

43
00:01:39,218 --> 00:01:41,338
and Citibank splits it and sends each

44
00:01:41,338 --> 00:01:42,880
lender its share.

45
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:45,010
The agent does not lend.

46
00:01:45,010 --> 00:01:46,870
It only moves the money.

47
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:49,424
But that means the agent's computers are

48
00:01:49,424 --> 00:01:51,740
connected to the bank's own vault.

49
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:53,656
If the agent sends too much,

50
00:01:53,656 --> 00:01:56,199
the extra comes out of Citibank's own

51
00:01:56,199 --> 00:01:57,261
pocket.

52
00:01:57,517 --> 00:01:59,040
By twenty twenty,

53
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:01,447
Revlon was in deep trouble.

54
00:02:01,587 --> 00:02:02,290
In May,

55
00:02:02,290 --> 00:02:04,714
it raised more than eight hundred million

56
00:02:04,714 --> 00:02:06,629
dollars in new financing,

57
00:02:06,629 --> 00:02:09,538
partly by moving assets that had protected

58
00:02:09,538 --> 00:02:12,077
the old loan into a new deal.

59
00:02:12,147 --> 00:02:14,527
Many lenders were furious.

60
00:02:14,617 --> 00:02:15,695
On the market,

61
00:02:15,695 --> 00:02:17,456
the old loan was trading for less

62
00:02:17,456 --> 00:02:19,817
than thirty cents on the dollar.

63
00:02:20,047 --> 00:02:21,141
In other words,

64
00:02:21,141 --> 00:02:23,596
investors doubted they would ever be paid

65
00:02:23,596 --> 00:02:24,866
back in full.

66
00:02:25,117 --> 00:02:25,684
Then,

67
00:02:25,684 --> 00:02:28,340
late on a Tuesday in August,

68
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:31,327
Citibank paid them back in full.

69
00:02:31,347 --> 00:02:32,991
By accident.

70
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:41,649
How do you send nine hundred million

71
00:02:41,649 --> 00:02:43,915
dollars by mistake?

72
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:50,020
The task looked simple.

73
00:02:50,250 --> 00:02:51,666
As part of a deal with some

74
00:02:51,666 --> 00:02:52,657
of its lenders,

75
00:02:52,657 --> 00:02:54,882
Revlon had agreed to pay interest to

76
00:02:54,882 --> 00:02:57,100
all of its old lenders early.

77
00:02:57,410 --> 00:02:59,323
Citibank's payment software,

78
00:02:59,323 --> 00:03:00,509
called Flexcube,

79
00:03:00,509 --> 00:03:02,320
had an awkward quirk.

80
00:03:02,630 --> 00:03:04,475
The easiest way to send only the

81
00:03:04,475 --> 00:03:06,936
interest was to enter the transaction as

82
00:03:06,936 --> 00:03:08,781
if Revlon were paying off the entire

83
00:03:08,781 --> 00:03:09,171
loan,

84
00:03:09,171 --> 00:03:11,631
and then redirect the principal into an

85
00:03:11,631 --> 00:03:12,841
internal account,

86
00:03:12,841 --> 00:03:14,256
called a wash account,

87
00:03:14,256 --> 00:03:16,101
so that the money never left the

88
00:03:16,101 --> 00:03:16,906
bank.

89
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:18,804
According to the court,

90
00:03:18,804 --> 00:03:21,201
Citibank's own manual said that to keep

91
00:03:21,201 --> 00:03:22,855
the principal inside,

92
00:03:22,855 --> 00:03:24,532
three fields on the screen all had

93
00:03:24,532 --> 00:03:27,050
to be set to the wash account.

94
00:03:27,370 --> 00:03:28,840
They were called front,

95
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:29,313
fund,

96
00:03:29,313 --> 00:03:30,910
and principal.

97
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:32,769
The employee who built the payment,

98
00:03:32,769 --> 00:03:34,335
part of a team in India working

99
00:03:34,335 --> 00:03:36,075
for Citibank through the outsourcing firm

100
00:03:36,075 --> 00:03:36,580
Wipro,

101
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:37,900
ticked only one.

102
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:39,902
Principal.

103
00:03:40,150 --> 00:03:43,058
Citibank had a safety rule for this,

104
00:03:43,058 --> 00:03:44,560
called six eyes.

105
00:03:44,670 --> 00:03:47,161
Three people must review a payment before

106
00:03:47,161 --> 00:03:48,440
it goes out.

107
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,650
The second person checked it and approved.

108
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:52,415
The third,

109
00:03:52,415 --> 00:03:55,887
a senior manager in the United States,

110
00:03:55,887 --> 00:03:56,612
replied,

111
00:03:56,612 --> 00:03:57,337
looks good,

112
00:03:57,337 --> 00:03:58,311
please proceed,

113
00:03:58,311 --> 00:04:00,660
principal is going to wash.

114
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:02,758
All three believed the screen meant

115
00:04:02,758 --> 00:04:04,857
something it did not.

116
00:04:05,117 --> 00:04:07,867
There was even a final warning.

117
00:04:07,867 --> 00:04:09,923
A box popped up saying the funds

118
00:04:09,923 --> 00:04:11,953
would be sent out of the bank,

119
00:04:11,953 --> 00:04:12,955
and asking,

120
00:04:12,955 --> 00:04:15,097
do you want to continue.

121
00:04:15,587 --> 00:04:17,967
But it did not say how much.

122
00:04:18,037 --> 00:04:20,623
And since the interest really was supposed

123
00:04:20,623 --> 00:04:21,679
to leave the bank,

124
00:04:21,679 --> 00:04:23,787
the warning looked normal.

125
00:04:23,927 --> 00:04:26,147
The approver clicked yes.

126
00:04:26,147 --> 00:04:28,152
And Citibank's money went out,

127
00:04:28,152 --> 00:04:28,806
matching,

128
00:04:28,806 --> 00:04:29,910
to the penny,

129
00:04:29,910 --> 00:04:32,989
everything Revlon owed on the loan.

130
00:04:33,250 --> 00:04:35,950
Even the evidence went unread.

131
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:36,983
That evening,

132
00:04:36,983 --> 00:04:38,395
as part of the routine,

133
00:04:38,395 --> 00:04:40,237
the employee who built the payment sent

134
00:04:40,237 --> 00:04:42,058
his manager the paperwork,

135
00:04:42,058 --> 00:04:44,500
including screenshots from the system.

136
00:04:44,750 --> 00:04:45,917
Under amount paid,

137
00:04:45,917 --> 00:04:47,697
they showed about seven point eight

138
00:04:47,697 --> 00:04:49,458
million dollars in interest,

139
00:04:49,458 --> 00:04:51,633
and eight hundred and ninety-four million

140
00:04:51,633 --> 00:04:53,420
dollars in principal.

141
00:04:53,750 --> 00:04:55,499
He did not see anything wrong,

142
00:04:55,499 --> 00:04:57,858
because he believed the system was showing

143
00:04:57,858 --> 00:04:59,823
the principal as paid into the bank's

144
00:04:59,823 --> 00:05:01,602
own internal account.

145
00:05:01,850 --> 00:05:04,074
Let me draw the size of the

146
00:05:04,074 --> 00:05:05,310
mistake.

147
00:05:05,370 --> 00:05:07,733
Here is what Citibank meant to send,

148
00:05:07,733 --> 00:05:10,710
about seven point eight million dollars.

149
00:05:10,710 --> 00:05:12,672
And here is what actually left the

150
00:05:12,672 --> 00:05:13,490
bank.

151
00:05:14,070 --> 00:05:16,514
The principal alone was almost nine

152
00:05:16,514 --> 00:05:18,580
hundred million dollars.

153
00:05:18,870 --> 00:05:21,310
The intended payment is barely a line

154
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:22,520
next to it.

155
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:25,220
More than a hundred times smaller.

156
00:05:25,483 --> 00:05:28,083
Nobody noticed that evening.

157
00:05:28,303 --> 00:05:29,466
The next morning,

158
00:05:29,466 --> 00:05:31,252
somebody did.

159
00:05:36,917 --> 00:05:39,337
Can you just ask for it back?

160
00:05:45,917 --> 00:05:47,679
Around nine the next morning,

161
00:05:47,679 --> 00:05:50,134
a team manager checking the previous day's

162
00:05:50,134 --> 00:05:52,365
accounts found a discrepancy that should

163
00:05:52,365 --> 00:05:53,857
not have been there.

164
00:05:54,097 --> 00:05:55,561
In a chat message,

165
00:05:55,561 --> 00:05:56,776
one employee wrote,

166
00:05:56,776 --> 00:05:57,496
bad news,

167
00:05:57,496 --> 00:05:59,481
the wires look like they went out

168
00:05:59,481 --> 00:06:00,577
the door.

169
00:06:00,767 --> 00:06:02,031
By the afternoon,

170
00:06:02,031 --> 00:06:04,816
Citibank was sending notices to every

171
00:06:04,816 --> 00:06:05,437
lender,

172
00:06:05,437 --> 00:06:07,151
saying the money had been sent in

173
00:06:07,151 --> 00:06:07,772
error,

174
00:06:07,772 --> 00:06:09,658
and asking for it back.

175
00:06:09,917 --> 00:06:12,247
Many lenders sent it back.

176
00:06:12,367 --> 00:06:14,800
About about three hundred and eighty-five

177
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:16,727
million dollars came home.

178
00:06:16,937 --> 00:06:18,501
But ten investment firms,

179
00:06:18,501 --> 00:06:20,996
managing funds that had received more than

180
00:06:20,996 --> 00:06:22,787
five hundred million dollars,

181
00:06:22,787 --> 00:06:24,067
refused.

182
00:06:24,267 --> 00:06:26,120
One of them replied that it was

183
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:27,741
not at all clear that the funds

184
00:06:27,741 --> 00:06:29,498
were paid in error.

185
00:06:29,750 --> 00:06:31,287
Inside the funds,

186
00:06:31,287 --> 00:06:33,770
the reactions were telling.

187
00:06:34,370 --> 00:06:36,880
Some sent the money straight back.

188
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:39,270
Others told their banks to wait.

189
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:41,627
One firm said it was working with

190
00:06:41,627 --> 00:06:44,223
its lawyers to get approval to return

191
00:06:44,223 --> 00:06:44,907
the funds,

192
00:06:44,907 --> 00:06:47,030
but asked its trustee to hold off

193
00:06:47,030 --> 00:06:48,810
until it heard back.

194
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:50,764
The longer they held on,

195
00:06:50,764 --> 00:06:53,140
the more it looked like a decision,

196
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:54,612
not a delay.

197
00:06:54,850 --> 00:06:56,790
Why would they keep it?

198
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:58,830
Look at it from their side.

199
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:00,695
They held a loan the market valued

200
00:07:00,695 --> 00:07:02,273
at under a third of its face

201
00:07:02,273 --> 00:07:03,070
value.

202
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,066
Suddenly,

203
00:07:04,066 --> 00:07:06,266
they had received every cent,

204
00:07:06,266 --> 00:07:07,310
in cash.

205
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:08,955
And they were already in a fight

206
00:07:08,955 --> 00:07:10,060
with Revlon.

207
00:07:10,060 --> 00:07:11,423
The very next day,

208
00:07:11,423 --> 00:07:13,502
a group of lenders sued Revlon and

209
00:07:13,502 --> 00:07:15,950
Citibank over the May deal.

210
00:07:16,150 --> 00:07:16,812
To them,

211
00:07:16,812 --> 00:07:18,970
this money was not a gift.

212
00:07:18,970 --> 00:07:20,960
It was what they were owed.

213
00:07:21,217 --> 00:07:23,991
So Citibank went to court,

214
00:07:23,991 --> 00:07:26,448
sure that the law was on its

215
00:07:26,448 --> 00:07:27,399
side.

216
00:07:33,050 --> 00:07:35,355
If someone pays you by mistake,

217
00:07:35,355 --> 00:07:37,768
do you have to give it back?

218
00:07:42,050 --> 00:07:43,212
Usually,

219
00:07:43,212 --> 00:07:43,950
yes.

220
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:45,220
Under New York law,

221
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:47,804
a payment made by mistake generally has

222
00:07:47,804 --> 00:07:49,480
to be returned.

223
00:07:49,490 --> 00:07:51,706
But there is an old exception,

224
00:07:51,706 --> 00:07:53,800
called discharge for value.

225
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:55,964
If you are owed money,

226
00:07:55,964 --> 00:07:58,342
and someone pays you that exact amount,

227
00:07:58,342 --> 00:08:00,306
and you had no reason to suspect

228
00:08:00,306 --> 00:08:01,157
a mistake,

229
00:08:01,157 --> 00:08:02,630
you can keep it.

230
00:08:02,890 --> 00:08:04,780
As New York's highest court once put

231
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:05,229
it,

232
00:08:05,229 --> 00:08:07,119
you should be able to treat the

233
00:08:07,119 --> 00:08:08,901
payment as final.

234
00:08:09,150 --> 00:08:11,160
Let me draw the test.

235
00:08:11,160 --> 00:08:12,390
First box.

236
00:08:12,390 --> 00:08:14,180
Were you owed this money?

237
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:15,300
Second box.

238
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:17,100
Did you do nothing to mislead the

239
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:18,000
sender?

240
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:19,050
Third box.

241
00:08:19,050 --> 00:08:20,554
Did you have no notice of a

242
00:08:20,554 --> 00:08:21,530
mistake?

243
00:08:21,630 --> 00:08:23,391
If the answer to all three is

244
00:08:23,391 --> 00:08:23,809
yes,

245
00:08:23,809 --> 00:08:26,170
the arrow leads to keeping it.

246
00:08:26,260 --> 00:08:27,863
If any answer is no,

247
00:08:27,863 --> 00:08:30,290
the arrow leads back to the sender.

248
00:08:30,430 --> 00:08:32,489
The whole case came down to that

249
00:08:32,489 --> 00:08:33,604
third box.

250
00:08:33,850 --> 00:08:35,999
After a trial in December,

251
00:08:35,999 --> 00:08:38,414
Judge Jesse Furman ruled in February

252
00:08:38,414 --> 00:08:39,980
twenty twenty-one.

253
00:08:40,150 --> 00:08:40,873
The lenders,

254
00:08:40,873 --> 00:08:41,410
he found,

255
00:08:41,410 --> 00:08:43,634
could reasonably have believed the payment

256
00:08:43,634 --> 00:08:45,160
was deliberate.

257
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:47,232
The amounts matched to the penny,

258
00:08:47,232 --> 00:08:49,445
and Revlon was backed by the billionaire

259
00:08:49,445 --> 00:08:50,632
Ronald Perelman,

260
00:08:50,632 --> 00:08:52,845
whose company owned about eighty-five

261
00:08:52,845 --> 00:08:54,250
percent of it.

262
00:08:54,490 --> 00:08:56,490
His conclusion was blunt.

263
00:08:56,530 --> 00:08:58,801
Citibank is not entitled to its money

264
00:08:58,801 --> 00:08:59,590
back.

265
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:02,085
The lenders could keep about about five

266
00:09:02,085 --> 00:09:04,626
hundred and four million dollars.

267
00:09:04,883 --> 00:09:05,991
For Citibank,

268
00:09:05,991 --> 00:09:08,252
it was an embarrassment on top of

269
00:09:08,252 --> 00:09:09,983
a bigger problem.

270
00:09:10,233 --> 00:09:12,319
The blunder raised questions about its

271
00:09:12,319 --> 00:09:12,924
controls,

272
00:09:12,924 --> 00:09:13,320
and,

273
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:15,406
as the trade publication Banking Dive

274
00:09:15,406 --> 00:09:16,011
noted,

275
00:09:16,011 --> 00:09:18,097
it may have contributed to the four

276
00:09:18,097 --> 00:09:19,974
hundred million dollars fine that its

277
00:09:19,974 --> 00:09:22,477
regulator had imposed on the bank in

278
00:09:22,477 --> 00:09:23,703
October.

279
00:09:24,093 --> 00:09:26,721
Citibank said it would appeal.

280
00:09:26,983 --> 00:09:29,789
The appeal took another year and a

281
00:09:29,789 --> 00:09:30,670
half.

282
00:09:36,317 --> 00:09:38,736
Should the lenders have known?

283
00:09:45,317 --> 00:09:46,667
On September eighth,

284
00:09:46,667 --> 00:09:48,017
twenty twenty-two,

285
00:09:48,017 --> 00:09:50,304
the federal appeals court in New York

286
00:09:50,304 --> 00:09:52,277
reversed the ruling.

287
00:09:52,377 --> 00:09:52,876
First,

288
00:09:52,876 --> 00:09:55,239
the loan was not due until twenty

289
00:09:55,239 --> 00:09:56,627
twenty-three.

290
00:09:56,627 --> 00:09:58,744
So the lenders were not owed that

291
00:09:58,744 --> 00:09:59,662
money yet,

292
00:09:59,662 --> 00:10:02,015
and the old exception did not protect

293
00:10:02,015 --> 00:10:02,850
them.

294
00:10:03,083 --> 00:10:03,790
Second,

295
00:10:03,790 --> 00:10:05,740
the court listed red flags that should

296
00:10:05,740 --> 00:10:08,665
have made any careful investor pick up

297
00:10:08,665 --> 00:10:09,753
the phone.

298
00:10:10,033 --> 00:10:12,336
Revlon had not sent the notice required

299
00:10:12,336 --> 00:10:13,773
for paying early.

300
00:10:13,983 --> 00:10:16,164
A struggling company was supposedly

301
00:10:16,164 --> 00:10:18,345
repaying everything three years ahead of

302
00:10:18,345 --> 00:10:19,143
time.

303
00:10:19,433 --> 00:10:20,790
It could have bought the same debt

304
00:10:20,790 --> 00:10:22,728
on the market for under thirty cents

305
00:10:22,728 --> 00:10:24,103
on the dollar.

306
00:10:24,423 --> 00:10:25,598
And days earlier,

307
00:10:25,598 --> 00:10:27,996
it had launched a deal designed to

308
00:10:27,996 --> 00:10:30,754
stop the loan from coming due early.

309
00:10:31,017 --> 00:10:33,557
The money had to go back.

310
00:10:33,557 --> 00:10:34,099
By then,

311
00:10:34,099 --> 00:10:36,517
the industry had already moved.

312
00:10:36,517 --> 00:10:39,146
Many loan contracts now include clauses

313
00:10:39,146 --> 00:10:41,512
saying that a payment made in error

314
00:10:41,512 --> 00:10:43,427
must be returned.

315
00:10:43,477 --> 00:10:44,781
And Revlon itself,

316
00:10:44,781 --> 00:10:46,991
the company at the center of it

317
00:10:46,991 --> 00:10:47,411
all,

318
00:10:47,411 --> 00:10:50,442
later filed for bankruptcy protection.

319
00:10:50,683 --> 00:10:52,676
So what does this story say about

320
00:10:52,676 --> 00:10:54,383
the money system?

321
00:10:54,553 --> 00:10:56,734
That the biggest transfers in the world

322
00:10:56,734 --> 00:10:58,916
still pass through a few people looking

323
00:10:58,916 --> 00:11:00,243
at a screen.

324
00:11:00,553 --> 00:11:02,566
Six eyes are only safe if each

325
00:11:02,566 --> 00:11:04,302
pair is really looking,

326
00:11:04,302 --> 00:11:06,663
not trusting the other two.

327
00:11:07,043 --> 00:11:08,836
A warning that does not show the

328
00:11:08,836 --> 00:11:11,453
amount is not much of a warning.

329
00:11:11,843 --> 00:11:14,179
And when money arrives that seems too

330
00:11:14,179 --> 00:11:15,324
good to be true,

331
00:11:15,324 --> 00:11:16,959
the law may one day ask a

332
00:11:16,959 --> 00:11:18,493
simple question.

333
00:11:18,583 --> 00:11:20,607
Why didn't you call?

334
00:11:20,850 --> 00:11:23,310
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

335
00:11:23,310 --> 00:11:26,244
we follow the money behind another hidden

336
00:11:26,244 --> 00:11:28,640
link of the world economy.

337
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:30,985
This is The Hidden Economics.
